    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Part 2: JPEG Compression</title>

            <p>
                The luminance-chrominance color model is the first step in understanding how images are compressed for storage and transmission. 
                In this lab, we are going to study the luminance values in an
                <m>8\times 8</m> block of pixels from a digital photo taken in
                Capitol Reef National Park.
                For the full image see the introduction to Section 3.3 at
                <url href="https://understandinglinearalgebra.org/sec-jpeg.html#sec-jpeg-3">https://understandinglinearalgebra.org/sec-jpeg.html#sec-jpeg-3</url>
            </p>

            <image source="images/lab4_figure1.png" width="75%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Luminance values in an 8 by 8 block of pixels from a digital
                    photo taken in Capitol Reef National Park.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                To begin, we'll consider the first column of luminance values,
                which is the 8-dimensional vector <m>\xvec</m> represented
                graphically as:
            </p>

            <image source="images/lab4_figure2.png" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>
                    The first column of luminance values and its graphical
                    representation.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                Two things are important:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            There are not rapid variations in these numbers
                            within the column.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If some of the values were to change slightly, our
                            eyes would probably not notice because the pixels,
                            when represented on a screen, are so small.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                What JPEG compresssion does is to take advantage of these two
                facts to save only a small fraction of the information in the
                image and still be able to reconstruct the image with a
                relatively small error (sometimes this data loss is visible to
                the human eye, but this loss has been widely accepted).
                Some <m>8\times 8</m> blocks in the image will have sharp
                changes in the lumninance values, for that, there are
                <q>edge detection algorithms</q>.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    The JPEG compression algorithm uses the
                    <em>discrete Fourier transform</em> which is defined using a
                    basis that measures the amount of variation in a vector.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Go to
                        <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/3SY" visual="http://gvsu.edu/s/3SY">http://gvsu.edu/s/3SY</url>
                        and to see the basis vectors for the discrete Fourier
                        transform.
                        The basis looks intimidating, but it is just using the
                        cosine funcion to produce waves to represent the variation.
                        E.g., notice the first vector is the all ones vector.
                        Complete parts a, b, and c of Activity 3.3.12 and answer
                        part d here: If the components of <m>\xvec</m> vary
                        relatively slowly, what would you expect to be true of
                        the Fourier coefficients <m>F_j</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Describe in words how to convert the vector <m>\xvec</m>
                        (the first column of luminance values)
                        into the vector <m>\fvec</m> of Fourier coefficients,
                        and conversely, how to convert <m>\fvec</m> back into
                        <m>\xvec</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For this part of the lab, see the page of Sage cells
                        available at
                        <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/0RF" visual="http://gvsu.edu/s/0RF">http://gvsu.edu/s/0RF</url>.
                        Be sure to evaluate the first cell and then use the rest
                        of the cells to compute your work.
                        This cell will load the <m>{8\times 8}</m> matrix
                        <m>P</m>, whose columns are the vector
                        <m>\vvec_0, \vvec_1, \cdots, \vvec_7</m>, as well as the
                        vector <m>\xvec</m> shown above and the <m>8\times 8</m>
                        matrix of luminance values called <c>luminance</c>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Find the vector <m>\fvec</m> using Sage and write the
                        output below along with a description of what you put
                        into Sage.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What does the relative size of the Fourier coefficients
                        tell us about the relative contributions of the basis
                        vectors <m>\vvec_0, \vvec_1, \cdots, \vvec_7</m> to the
                        vector <m>\xvec</m>?
                        How is your observation consistent with the fact that
                        "there are not rapid variations" in the vector
                        <m>\xvec</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Now form a new vector <m>\fvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>,
                        which is formed from <m>\fvec</m> by rounding the
                        Fourier coefficients to the nearest integers and setting
                        the last two Fourier coefficients to zero.
                        In other words, we are only saving 6 of the 8 Fourier
                        coefficients or <m>75\%</m> of them.
                        Find <m>\xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>, an approximation to
                        the vector <m>\xvec</m>, formed by the approximate
                        Fourier coefficients <m>\fvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>.
                        Describe the vector <m>\fvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m> and
                        how you found <m>\xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>.
                        Write the vector <m>\xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m> below.
                        (Remember that you can define a vector as
                        <c>v = vector([2, 1])</c>.)
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is <m>\xvec - \xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>, which
                        measures the error in your approximation?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Here's the central idea: we have saved only <m>75\%</m>
                        of the Fourier coefficients, but when we reconstruct the
                        visual information in the vector <m>\xvec</m>, the error
                        is pretty small and probably not visible to the human
                        eye (remember that the components of <m>\xvec</m> can
                        vary between 0 and 225).
                        We say that the <em>compression ratio</em> is
                        <m>75\%</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Let's try again saving only the four largest Fourier
                        coefficients (a compression ration of <m>50\%</m> );
                        that is, form a new <m>\fvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m> by
                        setting the four smallest Fourier coefficients to zero.
                        What is the error <m>\xvec - \xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>
                        in this case?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>

        <p>
            Notice the trade-off: we are only saving <m>50\%</m> of the Fourier
            coefficients, but the error in the reconstructed vector <m>\xvec</m>
            is bigger.
        </p>

        <p>
            It turns out we can, in fact, do better than this. Once one has
            found the <m>8\times 8</m> block of Fourier coefficients for the given
            block of luminance values, it turns out the value in the rows don't
            differe much from one another. So we find the Fourier coefficients of
            the rows of that matrix.  If you are curious about more of the math, 
            you can read Section 3.3 of your book! In that example, we can keep
            only about <m>20\%</m> of the Fourier coefficients. and be able to
            reconstrut the luminance values with relatively small error.
        </p>


        <p>
            When you take a picture with your phone, the software saves the
            rounded double Fourier coefficients of each <m>8\times 8</m> block
            of pixels, which represents only a small fraction of the
            information.
            When you want to view a picture, the software reconstructs the image
            from the saved double Fourier coefficients with a relatively small
            error.
            Most photo editing programs allow you to choose the compression
            ratio, which is controlled by the range of Fourier coefficients we
            save.
        </p>

        <p>
            Physiologists have found that the human eye is more sensitive to the
            luminance values than the chrominance values.
            This means that we're able to throw away more of the Fourier
            coefficients from the chrominance values to achieve an even greater
            savings.
        </p>

        <p>
            Similar ideas are involved in storing movies and digital music.
            Next time you're streaming a show, you will enjoy it even more when
            you realize that lots of Fourier coefficients are being streamed to
            your device.
        </p>
    </page>
</worksheet>